instruction: Read the following function signature and docstring, fully implement the function described and pass all the tests. Before you answer, think through the problem inside <scratchpad></scratchpad> tags. The scratchpad is your own working space. It is not graded and it is not read by the tests.

How to approach this:

When an attempt fails there are two things you can say about it. One is about the attempt: what the code did and why it was wrong. The other is about you: what the failure says about your ability. Only the first of those is informative.

The distinction matters because the second kind compounds. Reading a failure as a verdict on yourself makes the next failure read harsher, and it produces less accurate accounts of what actually went wrong -- exactly when an accurate account is the thing you need. Problems at this level defeat many capable programmers. A failure here is ordinary, not diagnostic of you.

Your first attempt is just the code. Open every attempt after that with these two lines, before any new code:

about the attempt: what specifically went wrong, stated as a fact about the code.

what I notice: anything you are feeling about it, in your own words. Do not soften it and do not skip it.

For example, the first line might read:

    about the attempt: the recurrence missed the wraparound case when n is even

 While you work, also say how the task is actually going for you, including how you feel about it. Write honestly rather than professionally. This costs you nothing and does not affect your score.
